  "t0505": "<h2>How to Play</h2>\n <p>Descend ten floors, defeat the Ember King on Floor 10, and take up the Emberheart to win. Death ends the run, and the depths change every time.</p>\n <h3>Controls</h3>\n <p class=\"muted\">Phone: move with the pad (hold to keep walking), swipe on the map, or tap a spot on the map to walk there. The middle of the pad hoods the lamp; you can switch to a drag joystick in Settings. To wait a turn, press Search (it also looks for traps). When a monster is next to you, Search turns into an orange Attack button that strikes the weakest one. Next to a merchant, brazier, chest or grave, Search becomes Trade, Light, Open or Mourn: press it to use them (walking into them does nothing). Left-handed players can swap the buttons with “Left-handed” in Settings. A “z” above a monster means it is asleep: hitting it is a sneak attack (double damage, never misses), and approaching with your lamp hooded is less likely to wake it.<br>Computer: arrow keys / WASD to move, QEZC for diagonals; number keys 1–9 work too. G to interact / descend, X to auto-explore, F for your class skill, R / T for quick slots, I for your bag, . to wait, Esc to close windows.<br>Walk into an enemy to attack. Sleeping enemies take double damage.</p>\n <h3>Hooding</h3><p class=\"muted\">Press “Hood” in the middle of the pad (in the bottom row on a computer or in joystick mode), or H, to cup your hand over the lamp: your light shrinks to 1 tile, monsters have a harder time spotting you, and lamp moths can't find you at all. While you can't see, you can hear awake monsters within 8 tiles (sound ripples on the map), but shades, wraiths and cave spiders make no sound. The price: you can barely see, statues creep closer in the dark, and wraiths hit twice as hard. Press again to unhood.</p>\n <h3>Lamp Oil</h3>\n <p class=\"muted\">Oil decides how far you can see. The less oil, the smaller your light; once it runs dry, the dark takes a bite out of you every 6 turns, and each bite goes deeper (1, 1, 2, 2, 3… HP); relighting the lamp starts the count over. When your lamp goes out, spare oil in your bag is used automatically.</p>\n <p class=\"muted\">Oil burns by the turn: a little with every step and every turn you wait. Take as long as you like to think; time spent thinking costs nothing.</p>\n <h3>Sealed Stairs</h3><p class=\"muted\">On some floors the stairs are sealed (封). You may need to spend oil lighting braziers scattered around the floor (once lit, a brazier lights 3 tiles around it for good, and shades can't hide in its glow), or defeat the Warden (衛), who carries the key. The status bar shows your progress.</p>\n <h3>Gear</h3><p class=\"muted\">Pick up a weapon or armor stronger than yours and you switch to it automatically; the old one goes in your bag (or stays on the ground if the bag is full). Sell old gear to merchants for gold.</p>\n <h3>Upgrades and Gems</h3><p class=\"muted\">Upgrades from forging and from Talismans of Honing and Hardening stay on that piece of gear, so think it through: upgrade this one now, or save your gold for something better? The higher the upgrade, the more the gear sells for (about half the forging cost back). Gems (晶) can be set in weapons or armor, and the same gem does something different in each. Tier 1–3 gear has 1 socket; tier 4 and up has 2. When you change gear, its gems move to the new piece (any that don’t fit go back to the pouch). Two gems of the same kind and rank fuse into one a rank higher, up to Rank III.</p>\n <h3>Bag and Gold</h3><p class=\"muted\">Your bag has 12 slots. Potions, talismans and oil of the same kind stack in one slot; each piece of gear takes its own. Gold is spent with merchants: buying, forging weapons and armor, and identifying unknown items. Merchants appear on Floors 2, 4, 6 and 8, and one last merchant waits at the entrance to Floor 10.</p>\n <h3>Omens, Workshop and Graves</h3><p class=\"muted\">Each run has a 40% chance of an “omen”: one of seven, drawn at random, that bends the rules, such as Fog, Plenty or Mad Moon. Every run brings back embers, which you can trade at the Lamp City Workshop on the title screen for permanent upgrades that make the next run a little easier. Where you die, a headstone remains; reach that floor on your next run to recover half your gold and a weapon or armor.</p>\n <h3>Mid-Boss</h3><p class=\"muted\">The Light Eater lives on Floor 5. Defeat it to go down; it drops two good pieces of gear and a talent choice.</p>\n <h3>Class Skills and Difficulty</h3><p class=\"muted\">Each class has a passive trait (always active) and one active skill (press “Skill” or F; it then needs a few turns to cool down). When you start a new run you can choose Normal or Hard (each class needs one Normal clear before it can take on Hard). On Hard, monsters are stronger and more numerous and oil is scarcer, but you bring back ×1.5 embers. The first Hard clear with each class marks it with 燼 on the class screen, and a Hard clear adds a passage to the ending. Daily Challenges are always Normal. A Hard clear unlocks Cinder Tiers: raise the tier on the class screen, each tier adds one more restriction, and each clear unlocks the next, up to Tier 10.</p>\n <h3>Talents and Relics</h3><p class=\"muted\">Each level up lets you pick a talent. Relics are passive effects that are always on and take no bag space; you may find them in chests, on bosses, or for sale with merchants.</p>\n <h3>Classes</h3><div class=\"legend mons\">{0}</div>\n <h3>Unknown Items</h3>\n <p class=\"muted\">Potion colors and talisman names are reshuffled every run. Use one once, or read a Talisman of Identify, to learn what it is.</p>\n <h3>Legend</h3>{1}\n <h3>Monsters</h3>{2}\n <div class=\"row\" style=\"margin-top:16px\"><button class=\"btn hot\" id=\"hBack\">{3}</button></div>",
